Durarara!! AU
Chapter 29 – Part 1
Curtis looked at the time.
“I gotta go back to class. I still can make the last—”
Celty’s hands hit his chest.
The umbrella coat folded. The cheap mattress took his weight. Cap knocked crooked. The phone and the bags stayed on the desk like a court that had already adjourned.
“Celty?”
Smoke filled the space above her collar, tight, not playful.
“Do me this one selfish favor. Please.”
Not hold me. Not plain language. The other ask. The one that had a motel door and a result still glowing in the room.
Curtis looked at her. At the clock. At a last class that would keep his cover if he ran.
He did not run.
“Okay, Celty.”
No speech. No condition. The same boy who had said why would I say no to you on a couch, answering a dullahan who had just learned her house was a lie and still chose this bed instead of a kitchen.
Her gloves found the tie.
The last period at Raira went on without its class rep.
On a motel mattress that would never be evidence,
time lost.
Celty Sturluson asked for selfish
and meant it.
Curtis Nightstar said okay
and let the answer on the phone
wait outside the favor.
Afterward, the motel remembered how to be cheap.
Celty lay on his chest, empty space above her collar tucked under his jaw, one arm thrown across the black shirt he had not fully escaped. Smoke thin. Not the angry ribbon from the results. The other kind. Spent.
Curtis stared at the water stain on the ceiling. Cap gone. Mask gone. Tie somewhere on the floor with the umbrella coat. His heartbeat was still trying to be a person who had a last class.
She did not speak first.
The private channel arrived small.
…Thank you.
Not for the slides. For staying when the clock said Raira.
His hand found her back. Slow. Present.
“You don’t have to thank me for a favor you asked for.”
I do. I used you to make the house quieter. That is selfish.
“I said okay.” A short breath into her smoke. “Okay isn’t a trap, Celty. It’s a door I opened on purpose.”
She pressed closer, listening to the heart she had felt racing under a blanket on a different night. This one was slower. Tired. Two hours of sleep and a balcony and a verdict, then this.
Shinra will smell nothing. I will smell nothing. You will go back to school like a student. A pause. I will go back to a kitchen that bought a helmet strap.
Curtis’s mouth tugged, not quite a smile.
“Last class already started without me. I’ll be the foreigner who vanished at lunch. Anri’s going to invent a hospital. Rio’s going to invent a club.”
Celty’s fingers curled in his shirt.
Let them. For a minute I get to be here. Not a courier. Not a woman photographing her own desk.
He held her.
The bags were still on the table. The phone was still a verdict. Ikebukuro was still full of a scarred girl and a pen and a first-year’s kettle.
None of it got the bed yet.
On a mattress that charged by the hour,
afterward was just weight
and a chest
and smoke.
Celty Sturluson laid her absence
where it fit.
Curtis Nightstar missed last period
and did not move her
off the only quiet
the day had allowed.
They both got ready like the room had a checkout time and a city attached.
Coat. Cap. Mask looped to one ear. Helmet. Gloves. The bags went into the kit. The verdict went into a pocket that would not open in a kitchen.
Celty stopped at the door.
“I’ll text you later.”
“Okay, Celty.”
A beat. Smoke ticked.
“Curtis?”
“Yeah. What is it?”
“Can you kiss my neck again before we both leave.”
He walked up.
No joke. No clock. Hands at the line of her collar where a pulse would have been, mouth on the skin that was still hers. The kiss was not a peck for a hallway. It was the leftover of the favor — slow, certain, the kind that said I missed you without putting it back into words.
He drew back only far enough to see the empty space above her collar.
“See ya soon, Celty.”
She stood there half a second longer, smoke thin, then the helmet went on and she was the Black Rider again.
Curtis pulled the mask up. The umbrella coat made him a wrong scientist in a motel parking lot.
They left by different doors.
On a threshold that still smelled like cheap soap and a result,
a neck got the last honest thing in the room.
Celty Sturluson asked and was answered.
Curtis Nightstar kissed her like later
was already a promise and not a maybe.
The last bell had already spent itself.
Curtis came through the front gate in uniform like a man arriving at a fire after the smoke. Bike home. Coat off. Mask off. Cap off. Student back on. Sprint that still lost to a clock.
He stopped under the entrance overhang, hands on his knees for one useless second, and told the pavement the only prayer he had left.
“Fuck all kinds of duck.”
“Curtis-kun!”
Anri was already moving. Bag half-open. Face the kind of pale that meant she had counted desks and come up short.
He straightened too fast. Braids swung. The two-hour sleep and the balcony and the motel tried to stand in a straight line and failed.
“Anri-chan.” A breath. A grin that did not reach both eyes. “Please tell me last period was a myth.”
“It wasn’t.” She stopped in front of him, close enough to see the run still in his chest. “You weren’t in the hall. You weren’t on the roof. Rio said you went to smoke and then you were gone.” Her voice dropped. “I thought— the lake. Or the hotel. Or you fell again.”
Curtis winced.
“I didn’t fall in a lake. I missed a bell. That’s all you’re getting in a doorway.” He tipped his head toward the emptying school. “Teacher write me up?”
“Not yet. I said you felt sick.” The lie sat awkwardly in her mouth, like she had borrowed it from Mikado’s toolkit and hated the fit. “Curtis-kun. You look like you ran through the whole city.”
He had.
He did not say a balcony. He did not say a kit. He did not say a neck.
“Yeah,” he said. “I kind of did.”
On a Raira gate after the last class,
a class rep came back too late.
Anri Sonohara found him
and used his name like a net.
Curtis Nightstar had changed twice,
raced twice,
and still lost the bell —
with fuck all kinds of duck
the only honest thing
he put in the air.
Curtis rubbed the back of his neck, eyes on the pavement instead of her.
“It’s a long story, Anri-chan. But I’m sorry. I’m supposed to be a class rep with you and all I have done is let you down.”
Anri’s fingers tightened on her bag strap.
The words class rep landed smaller than let you down. She had watched him pull her away from Nasujima. She had watched him take a board and a knife and a stare in a street fight. She had spent a night counting hours when he disappeared. A missed last period was not the ledger she was keeping.
“…You didn’t let me down.”
Soft. Firm enough to be a refusal.
“You fell out of a desk this morning. You didn’t go home. You drank a can for lunch and vanished off a roof. I was scared. That isn’t the same as you failing a title.” She glanced at the emptying hall, then back. “If you need the long story to stay long, I can wait. I just— don’t apologize like the only thing I wanted from you today was attendance.”
Curtis’s mouth tugged. Tired. Almost grateful. Almost guilty for a different reason she was not allowed to have.
“You’re too decent for this job, Anri-chan.”
“Then stop making me invent sick days.” A small breath that might have been a smile if she let it. “Can you walk? Or do I have to watch you fall out of the gate too.”
“I can walk.”
He fell into step beside her like a class rep who had arrived after the class.
On a school that had already ended,
an apology tried to be about a title.
Anri Sonohara would not take it
as the whole debt.
Curtis Nightstar said sorry
for the part she could see
and carried the rest
in a pocket
that still smelled
like a motel lamp.
“Hey, Anri-chan. Curtis. Wait up!”
Rio came up the walk like she had been hunting the same missing desk. Bag on one shoulder. Eyes going from Anri’s face to Curtis’s uniform to the fact that he was breathing like a sprint that had lost.
She fell in on his other side without asking.
“You vanished off a roof. I asked if he’d even accept food and then you weren’t a person anymore.” A look at his shoes, still faintly wrong from alley grit. “You didn’t make last period. Anri covered you. You’re welcome.”
Curtis tipped his head, braids shifting.
“Thanks. Both of you. I wasn’t trying to turn lunch into a missing-person report.”
Rio snorted.
“Too late. You have a face that makes people count chairs.” She glanced at Anri, then away, like the Anri-chan was still a new tool she refused to drop. “You look worse than this morning. That’s impressive. This morning you were already a disaster.”
Anri’s voice stayed quiet.
“He said it’s a long story.”
“Then it can be long after we move. Standing in a gate making him apologize is how he disappears again.” Rio jerked her chin toward the street. “Walk. If you’re going to be a problem, be a problem with witnesses.”
Curtis huffed something that wanted to be a laugh.
“Yes, ma’am.”
They walked. Class rep in the middle. Two girls who had argued over sandwiches and now shared the same late boy.
On a sidewalk after the bell,
the roof club became an escort.
Rio Kamichika caught up
and refused to let him
be sorry in one place.
Anri Sonohara kept the long story
unopened.
Curtis Nightstar walked between them
with a motel still in his pulse
and a school day
that had ended
without him.
They reached the gate.
Youko was already there. Brown hair. The same girl who had stood behind makeup and a yellow-hoodie boy and watched a knife become a haircut. She had brought a replacement.
The man beside her wore a pompadour like it was a weapon he polished. Takeshi. One of her boyfriends. The kind of Ikebukuro extra who showed up when a phone got smashed or a foreigner did not know his place.
Youko’s smile was all teeth.
“Well. Look who learned how to use the front door.” Her eyes flicked past Anri, past Rio, and stuck on Curtis. “Hiroshi still can’t grow his hair. Funny. I thought you’d be expelled by now.”
Takeshi cracked his neck, looking Curtis up and down like height was an insult.
“This the guy? The one who played barber?” A laugh that wanted a crowd. “Youko said he talks big. Doesn’t look like much in a uniform.”
Rio stepped half a foot forward without being asked.
“You already used your one hallway. Walk.”
Youko ignored her. Anri’s name was the one that mattered. In another version of this gate it would have been Mikado they boxed in. Today it was the quiet girl and the boy who had called them not his type.
Takeshi’s pompadour caught the late light.
“We’re looking for someone. Little info broker. Dark hair. Shitty grin. You three go to Raira. You see Orihara, you say so.” His chin tipped at Anri. “Especially you. You walk around like you don’t hear rumors.”
Curtis stopped under the gate like the day had not already spent him.
The motel was still in his pulse. The last class was still missed. He looked at the pompadour, then at Youko, and the tired in his face did not make him smaller.
On a Raira gate that canon had already used,
the extras arrived on schedule.
Youko brought Takeshi
and an old grudge with a new haircut.
Anri Sonohara, Rio Kamichika,
and Curtis Nightstar
stood in the place
Mikado was supposed to occupy —
with Izaya’s name
about to turn a sidewalk
into a problem.
Curtis blinked once, slow, like the pompadour had been a commercial.
“Sorry. I was zoning out. You say something, Elvis?”
Takeshi’s grin died mid-flex.
“The fuck did you just call me?”
Rio made a sound that wanted to be a laugh and had the manners not to finish it.
Youko’s face went sharp.
“Don’t play stupid. You heard him. Izaya. The information broker. He smashed my phone. You were in the street when Hiroshi—” She cut herself off, then pointed at Anri like a witness. “She was there. You were there. Don’t act like Ikebukuro is too big.”
Takeshi stepped in, chest first, pompadour leading.
“Say it again. I dare you. You cut a man’s hair in public and now you do jokes? You think a school uniform makes you untouchable?”
Curtis looked at him the way he had looked at Hiroshi’s knife. Tired. Unimpressed. The motel still in his bones and none of it for this.
“I think a haircut that tall is a cry for help.” A small tilt of his head. “And I think if your whole plan is ‘ask three students at a gate,’ Izaya already won.”
Anri’s hand found the strap of her bag. Not Saika. Not yet. Just the reflex of a girl who had been cornered before.
Youko’s smile came back thinner.
“Hiroshi said you zoned out then too. Right before you took his knife.” She flicked a look at Takeshi. “Don’t let him talk. He does that until people look stupid.”
Takeshi’s fist bunched.
“Then he can look at me.”
On a gate that wanted a fight,
Elvis got a name.
Takeshi heard it
and forgot Izaya
for a second.
Curtis Nightstar zoned back in
just enough to make a pompadour personal.
Curtis’s mouth barely moved.
“The only stupid person here is you with your clown makeup.”
Takeshi punched him.
The sound was clean. Knuckle to cheek. A gate punch, meant for a student.
Curtis’s head turned with it the polite amount and came back like the polite amount had been a suggestion. He did not step. He did not blink long.
“And don’t even get me started on the shitty phone brands you choose out.” He looked at Takeshi. The fist was still there, lingering like it wanted credit. “Also take your hand off my face. I’m not into guys.”
Takeshi’s pompadour held. His hand did not immediately remember how to leave.
Rio inhaled through her teeth.
Anri’s voice cut small and sharp.
“Stop.”
Youko laughed once, too high.
“He didn’t even move. Takeshi—”
“I moved,” Curtis said, mild. “Your boyfriend’s just light.” He caught Takeshi’s wrist with two fingers and set it aside like a cup on a table. “Izaya smashed a phone. That’s a him problem. You swinging on a class rep in a school gate is a you problem. Walk away before the walk becomes a haircut.”
Takeshi’s ears went red.
“You think this is funny?”
“I think you punched me and I still have better things to be late for.” Curtis’s mismatched eyes flicked to Youko. “Hiroshi learned. You can learn cheaper.”
On a Raira gate, a punch
arrived and found steel.
Takeshi’s hand stayed a second too long.
Curtis Nightstar called the makeup and the phone
and the orientation and left the fist with nothing to hold.
Curtis looked at Rio. Then Anri.
“I won’t break your spine. But I’ll give you one more warning. Walk away. And don’t insult Anri-chan or Rio.”
Takeshi did not take it.
The jacket went first. Fabric tore at the shoulder with a cheap school-store sound. Buttons popped. Raira blue became a rag in a pompadour’s fist.
Youko clapped once, delighted.
“There. Not so tall now.”
Curtis looked down at the rip.
The motel left his face. The missed bell left his face. What stayed was the boy who had counted shirts after a stabbing and called cheap metal a nice cut.
“…You ripped the uniform.”
Quiet.
Rio’s voice went flat.
“Takeshi. You are very stupid.”
Anri had not moved. Her hand had gone white on the bag strap.
Takeshi shook the torn flap like a trophy.
“Warning. Cute. You talk like you run the street. You’re a transfer in a costume. Next time you cut somebody’s hair, remember this—”
Curtis lifted his eyes.
Mismatched. Not tired anymore.
“I said one more warning.” He rolled the ruined shoulder once, testing the tear. “That was it. You don’t get a third. And you don’t get their names in your mouth.”
The gate had students at the far end. Not enough. Not none.
On a ripped Raira jacket,
the cheap lesson ended.
Takeshi held fabric
like he’d won.
Curtis Nightstar had promised
not to break a spine — which left every other option
on the table.
Curtis picked Takeshi up by the collar.
The pompadour rose with him. Toes left the pavement. The ripped jacket hung off Curtis’s shoulder like a flag.
Then the slaps started.
Open hand. Left. Right. The sound was ugly and ordinary. Not a punch. Not a street-sign. A correction.
“Apologize.”
Slap.
Takeshi swung and hit air.
“Apo—”
Slap.
“—logize.”
Youko screamed his name like volume could put him down.
Rio did not look away. Anri’s eyes were wide, not with the street-fight stare from the bucket night — this was smaller, meaner, a boy being treated like a door that wouldn’t shut.
Students at the far gate had phones half-up and the good sense not to come closer.
Takeshi’s cheek went red in layers. The pompadour listed.
“I’m— I’m sorry—!”
Curtis did not set him down yet.
“To them. Not to the air.” Another slap, lighter, like a metronome. “Anri-chan. Rio. The jacket you can replace. Their names you don’t get.”
Takeshi’s feet kicked once.
“I’m sorry! I’m sorry I ripped it— I’m sorry— girls— I’m sorry!”
Youko grabbed at Curtis’s arm and found an arm that did not notice her.
On a school gate, a pompadour
learned gravity.
Takeshi said sorry
because slaps
were a language
he understood.
Curtis Nightstar held him off the ground and collected the apology
he had promised instead of a spine.
Curtis’s glare cut sideways without dropping Takeshi.
“You’re next if you grab me like that again. I hate girls like you.”
Youko’s hands left his sleeve like the fabric had burned.
The smile was gone. What sat there instead was the same look she’d worn in the hallway when he pointed at Anri and called the rest of them makeup and a shitty attitude.
“You—” Her voice cracked, then found cruelty again. “You think you’re better because you’re foreign? Because you can pick him up? Hiroshi’s going to—”
“Hiroshi already had a turn.” Curtis set Takeshi down hard enough to make the pompadour bounce. “You hide behind boys and phones and other people’s names. That’s the part I hate. Not the hair. Not the brand.” He flicked a look at the hand that had grabbed him. “Touch me again and I stop being polite about it.”
Rio’s mouth was a thin line. Approval, ugly and honest.
Anri did not look triumphant. She looked at Youko the way she looked at Nasujima — like a shape that used people as cover.
Youko backed up a step. Then another. Pride trying to remember how to stand.
Takeshi held his face with both hands, apology still leaking out of him.
“Let’s go,” Youko snapped, and it was for him, not for Curtis. “This isn’t over.”
Curtis adjusted the ripped jacket on his shoulder.
“It is if you’re smart. Most of you aren’t.”
On a gate that had already taken a punch,
Youko learned the other warning.
Curtis Nightstar did not raise a hand
to her.
He named the kind of girl
he would not let
grab him twice.